{{/* settings.permissions.json — the permission model, spliced into
    settings.base.json as the value of its "permissions" key. Split out because it was
    43% of a 1284-line file; nothing about the rules changed in the move.

    THIS FILE IS POLICY. Four gates have to keep seeing it, and each fails silently
    rather than loudly if it stops:

      * modify_settings.json.sh.tmpl refuses to generate settings.json from a dirty
        tree. Its pathspec is a wildcard over .chezmoitemplates/settings.*.json so a
        new fragment is covered on creation; do not narrow it back to a list.
      * bin/config-soak tracks this path, so a change here needs review + `config-soak
        land` before it can ship.
      * tests/settings/settings-permissions-no-spawners.test.js and
        tests/secret-registry.test.js read this file's RAW TEXT and parse the allow/deny
        arrays out of it. They anchor on finding the arrays first, so moving rules out of
        here fails them loudly.
      * tests/settings/settings-templates-tracked.test.js asserts the three above still
        point at every fragment.

    Rendered as a bare object, so it starts with { and ends with } — not a fragment of
    one. Keep it that way: the base template supplies the key name and the comma. */}}
{
    "allow": [
      "Read(**)",
      "Glob",
      "Grep",
      {{/* Sessions manage their own worktrees end to end — create, enter, clean up — so none of
           that should ever stop for a prompt. This is a tool rule, not a Bash one, so unlike
           every Bash entry below it survives autoMode.classifyAllShell, which suspends the Bash
           allow list while auto mode is active. The Bash half of the same workflow
           (`git worktree add/remove`, `git branch -D`) is handled by NOT ask-listing it. */}}
      "EnterWorktree",
      "ExitWorktree",
      "Bash(ls:*)",
      "Bash(pwd)",
      "Bash(cd:*)",
      "Bash(test:*)",
      "Bash([:*)",
      "Bash(command -v:*)",
      "Bash(command -V:*)",
      "Bash(bash --version)",
      "Bash(/bin/bash --version)",
      {{/* the version probe only. `env` takes a command as its argument, so any wildcard here
           auto-approves `/usr/bin/env bash -c '<anything>'`; a bare `Bash(env)` was the same
           hole one level up (`env FOO=bar bash -c …`) and is deliberately absent below. */}}
      "Bash(/usr/bin/env bash --version)",
      "Bash(cat:*)",
      "Bash(head:*)",
      "Bash(tail:*)",
      "Bash(wc:*)",
      "Bash(file:*)",
      "Bash(stat:*)",
      "Bash(tree:*)",
      "Bash(find:*)",
      "Bash(grep:*)",
      "Bash(rg:*)",
      "Bash(fd:*)",
      "Bash(which:*)",
      "Bash(type:*)",
      "Bash(echo:*)",
      "Bash(date)",
      "Bash(whoami)",
      "Bash(uname:*)",
      "Bash(open:*)",
      "Bash(pbcopy)",
      "Bash(pbpaste)",
      "Bash(base64:*)",
      "Bash(xxd:*)",
      "Bash(shasum:*)",
      "Bash(md5:*)",
      "Bash(df:*)",
      "Bash(du:*)",
      {{/* `Bash(timeout:*)` was here to stop timeout-wrapped commands prompting (73892e2).
           It cannot stay: timeout's next token IS an arbitrary command, so the prefix rule
           auto-approved `timeout 5 bash -c '<anything>'` — the same hole as the `env`
           wrapper above, and deny globs can't cover it without enumerating every
           interpreter. The convenience comes back safely through claude_guard.judge's own
           `unwrap_wrapper`, which unwraps a leading wrapper (timeout/env/nice/stdbuf/
           nohup/setsid/xargs) and matches the command it wraps, so `timeout 200 node
           --test` is allowed because `node --test` is. */}}
      "Bash(history)",
      "Bash(man:*)",
      "Bash(less:*)",
      "Bash(more:*)",
      "Bash(diff:*)",
      "Bash(cmp:*)",
      "Bash(sort:*)",
      "Bash(uniq:*)",
      "Bash(cut:*)",
      "Bash(awk:*)",
      "Bash(sed:*)",
      "Bash(jq:*)",
      {{/* jsonq exists so JSON parsing is available without a permission prompt, and the blanket allow is the whole point of it. The grant was withdrawn 2026-07-29 (A7-01) because the tool validated an AST allowlist and then called eval on the original source — the checked language and the executed language were never the same thing, so exposed stdlib modules reached sys and builtins through ordinary non-underscore attribute chains. It is restored here because the thing it guarded no longer exists.
           What makes this defensible is an absence, not a check: no file in the tool contains eval, exec or compile. ast.parse builds a tree, which executes nothing; the tree is then walked by the interpreter in share/jsonq/interp.py, whose dispatch tables ARE the grammar, so a node type with no handler cannot run because nothing exists that would run it. ast.Attribute has no handler, so x.y is refused in every position — which closes the class rather than narrowing it, because with no attribute access the reachable object graph is exactly JSON values, a fixed function table, and query-defined lambdas, leaving no route from a value to any other object. --script runs on the same interpreter, not on eval.
           Do NOT take a green test suite as the warrant. The PREVIOUS suite was a deliberate 20-case escape suite, it was green, and the tool was fully escapable the entire time it was — a passing deny-list suite is evidence about the cases someone thought of and nothing else. The warrant is the structure block in tests/jsonq.test.js, which asserts the property itself: no eval/exec/compile anywhere in the source, no ast.Attribute entry in any dispatch table, and the escape-prone builtins (type, getattr, vars, globals, dir, open, format, super) absent from --functions. If a change ever reintroduces eval or gives ast.Attribute a handler, this rule comes back out in the same commit.
           The tool is bin/jsonq plus the nine modules in share/jsonq, and the grant covers all ten, so the structure block reads every one of them — discovered from disk, not listed, because a block that named its files would go quiet the moment a tenth appeared. Two further assertions exist because splitting the tool created the risk: every module imports only the standard library or a sibling, and nothing touches sys.path. The second is the sharp one. share/jsonq on sys.path would let an unmanaged json.py dropped there shadow the stdlib for every later import and run under this rule; the shim binds the directory to a package __path__ instead, which resolves _jsonq.* and nothing else, so an unexpected file is inert rather than a shadow. A test also pins that the directory holds exactly the modules the shim loads.
           Residual risk is runaway work, not escape: SIGALRM cannot interrupt a C-level operation, so the caps on **, <<, sequence repetition, range(), int() and format-spec widths are load-bearing. A catastrophically backtracking regex can still hang the CLI past --timeout; that is documented in the tool and costs a Ctrl-C, not a bypass. */}}
      "Bash(jsonq:*)",
      {{/* stdio-blocking exists so the homelab server repo's uv-python.sh hook can clear
           O_NONBLOCK on the Bash tool's stdio without its prefix landing on the
           `Bash(python3 -c:*)` ask rule — that inline form is arbitrary code execution and
           belongs there, but the whole point of this fixup is that it runs before EVERY
           ansible invocation, so an ask rule on it prompted for the whole compound chain
           (64 prompts in 7 days, 16 of them read-only shapes). The blanket allow is
           defensible for the same reason jsonq's is: an absence, not a check. The script
           takes no arguments (a stray one is a usage error, checked before anything else
           runs) and touches nothing but os.set_blocking on fds 0, 1 and 3 of its own
           process — no path, no network, no subprocess, nothing caller-controlled reaches
           it because there is nothing for caller data to modify. tests/stdio-blocking.test.js
           pins both: that an argument is refused, and that the fds it targets end up
           blocking. */}}
      "Bash(stdio-blocking)",
      {{/* otelq queries the local Loki/Prometheus stack. curl is ask-listed because it takes an arbitrary URL and can write the response to disk; otelq exposes neither — the host is a hardcoded 127.0.0.1 constant, not a flag, the method is always GET, and output only goes to stdout. LogQL/PromQL cannot spawn a process or read a file, so a free-form query argument stays inert. tests/otelq.test.js pins the loopback bases, the absence of any --host/--url/--output flag, and the GET-only construction. */}}
      "Bash(otelq:*)",
      {{/* otel-sweep is otelq's question asked of every machine, and it earns the same grant the same way — by absence. ssh is ask-listed because it takes a destination and a command, both arbitrary; otel-sweep exposes neither. The destinations are a three-entry dict of literals and --only validates against that dict, so no argument names a host. The program that runs on each machine is PROBE, one module-level constant delivered on stdin to `python3 -`; nothing is ever interpolated into it or into any argv, so there is no position where caller data becomes a command. subprocess is always given an argv list and never shell=True, so there is no shell to escape. There is no --host, --url, --query or --output flag, every request is a GET, and PROBE writes no files — output is JSON on stdout.
           The weak link is worth naming rather than hiding: on daniel-server Loki is not published on the host, so PROBE resolves the container's bridge address out of `docker inspect` and builds a URL from it. That is the one place remote data selects a network target. It is constrained by construction — the value must match an RFC1918 literal or it is discarded, and the port is a constant — so the reachable set is private addresses on that machine's own docker bridge and nothing else.
           tests/otel-sweep.test.js pins the properties rather than sampling bad inputs: the destination enum, the absence of every redirecting flag, no shell=True or bare eval/exec/compile, PROBE never concatenated or formatted, the ssh argv built from constants with BatchMode=yes, the RFC1918 gate, and that the probe opens no files and sends no request body. If a --host flag or an interpolated command ever lands, this rule comes out in the same commit. */}}
      "Bash(otel-sweep:*)",
      {{/* stash-mine exists because `git stash pop`/`drop`/`clear` are ask-listed for a reason a classifier cannot see: the stash stack is shared across every worktree of a repo, and a bare pop applied another session's 25-file WIP into a tree once. The prescribed protocol — push with a unique tag, capture the SHA, apply/drop by that SHA, never pop — closes the hole, but only if every write path actually checks ownership; stash-mine is that protocol with nothing left optional.
           It earns the grant by the same absence as its siblings above: no --path, --ref, --worktree or --repo flag exists, so the only caller-supplied value that ever reaches a git command is a stash SHA, and apply/drop both re-validate that SHA against THIS worktree's own tag (read fresh from `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` and the current branch, never from argv) before using it — find_own() exits rather than acting on a SHA that resolves to no entry or to another worktree's entry. There is no `pop` subcommand (apply and drop are separate, so a bad apply never costs the entry) and no `clear` (it cannot be scoped to one worktree). drop re-reads stash@{n} for the target SHA immediately before dropping, so a concurrent push from another worktree — which can only insert above index 0, never remove an entry this tool doesn't own — cannot shift the wrong entry under it. subprocess is always given an argv list and never shell=True.
           tests/stash-mine.test.js pins the structure (the four-subcommand closed set, no pop/clear/shell=True, the tag built from git rather than argv, the re-resolve-at-drop-time call) and then drives the real tool against a temp repo with two worktrees: a foreign SHA is refused for both apply and drop, an own SHA works, and dropping by SHA survives a foreign push landing between list and drop. If a --path/--ref/--worktree flag or a `pop`/`clear` subcommand ever lands, this rule comes out in the same commit. */}}
      "Bash(stash-mine:*)",
      "Bash(yq:*)",
      {{/* xargs is NOT allow-listed, for the same reason env isn't: it takes a command as
           an argument, so no prefix of it is safe. It used to be allowed with `xargs *sh*`
           and `xargs *bash*` denies standing guard, and those were wrong in both directions
           — they missed every non-shell interpreter (`xargs python -c`, `node -e`, `perl -e`,
           `ruby -e` all auto-approved, measured 2026-07-29), while a substring glob also
           matched an ordinary FILENAME, so `xargs grep foo build.sh` was refused for
           containing "sh". A glob cannot tell the command xargs runs from a path it is
           merely handed, and this rule is the only defence for a bare `xargs` invocation
           with no command of its own to unwrap.
           The everyday pipeline is not lost: `… | xargs wc -l` resolves through
           claude_guard.judge's `unwrap_wrapper`, which judges `wc -l` in xargs's place.
           Bare `xargs …` with nothing after it to unwrap still prompts. */}}
