           by the local Read deny rules above, so a secret there is readable without a prompt. */}}
      "mcp__homelab__host_overview",
      "mcp__homelab__service_health",
      "mcp__homelab__container_status",
      "mcp__homelab__list_containers",
      "mcp__homelab__container_logs",
      "mcp__homelab__top_containers",
      "mcp__homelab__query_logs",
      "mcp__homelab__query_metric",
      "mcp__homelab__scrape_targets",
      "mcp__homelab__disk_health",
      "mcp__homelab__cert_expiry",
      "mcp__homelab__ha_state",
      "mcp__homelab__claude_code_usage",
      "mcp__homelab__claude_code_events",
      "mcp__homelab__list_files",
      "mcp__homelab__read_file",
      "Bash(~/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-plugins-official/ralph-loop/*)",
      "Bash(bash ~/.claude/hooks/session-context.sh)",
      "Bash(~/.claude/hooks/block-dangerous-bash.sh)",
      "Skill(keybindings-help)",
      {{/* Dependabot PR triage. The agent pushes branches, opens combined PRs and closes the
           superseded originals, so this is not a read-only grant — it is allowed because the
           work is repetitive and the agent's own brief carries the guardrails (draft PRs,
           never force-push a dependabot branch, merge nothing). Named specifically rather
           than a bare Agent rule, which would auto-approve every subagent type. */}}
      "Agent(lpt-dependabot-reviewer:dependabot-reviewer)"
    ],
    "deny": [
      "WebFetch(domain:github.com)",
      "Read(**/.env)",
      "Read(**/.env.*)",
      "Read(**/*.pem)",
      "Read(**/*.key)",
      "Read(**/*.p12)",
      "Read(**/*.pfx)",
      "Read(**/*.secret)",
      "Read(**/id_rsa)",
      "Read(**/id_ed25519)",
      "Read(**/.ssh/**)",
      "Read(**/.aws/credentials)",
      "Read(**/.aws/config)",
      "Read(~/.gnupg/**)",
      "Read(~/.netrc)",
      "Read(~/.pypirc)",
      "Read(~/.npmrc)",
      "Read(**/secrets/**)",
      {{/* The SOPS age private keys. The `*.key` deny above does NOT reach them — the file is
           literally named keys.txt — and nothing else in this list covers .agekey. Losing or
           leaking these is the one failure with no recovery path: they decrypt every secret in
           a PUBLIC repo, and .gitignore blocking the commit does not stop a read that pastes
           one into a transcript. Denied outright rather than ask-listed, because an ask is a
           prompt and prompts here get approved 99.6% of the time. */}}
      "Read(~/.config/sops/age/**)",
      "Read(**/*.agekey)",
      "Edit(~/.config/sops/age/**)",
      "Edit(**/*.agekey)",
      "Edit(**/.env)",
      "Edit(**/.env.*)",
      "Edit(**/*.pem)",
      "Edit(**/*.key)",
      "Edit(**/*.p12)",
      "Edit(**/*.pfx)",
      "Edit(**/*.secret)",
      "Edit(**/id_rsa)",
      "Edit(**/id_ed25519)",
      "Edit(**/.ssh/**)",
      "Edit(**/.aws/credentials)",
      "Edit(**/.aws/config)",
      "Edit(~/.gnupg/**)",
      "Edit(~/.netrc)",
      "Edit(~/.pypirc)",
      "Edit(~/.npmrc)",
      "Edit(**/secrets/**)",
      {{/* Costs nothing today and closes the door tomorrow. Plain `kubectl` authenticates as
           system:serviceaccount:kube-system:homelab-readonly, which holds get/list/watch — so
           `kubectl auth can-i delete pods` already answers "no", as does delete pvc and
           pods/exec. With `sudo` denied just below, Ansible is the ONLY write path to this
           cluster. This entry means a future credential widening cannot silently hand back
           `delete` against Longhorn PVCs and their B2 backup chain. Deny rather than
           autoMode.soft_deny: a soft block clears on explicit user intent, and "delete that
           pod" is exactly the phrasing that would clear it.

           Per-verb matching means this cannot carve out the routine `delete pod` (the
           Deployment recreates it) — accepted, because RBAC refuses that too today. */}}
      "Bash(kubectl delete:*)",
      "Bash(sudo:*)",
      "Bash(su:*)",
      "Bash(chown:*)",
      "Bash(chmod 777:*)",
      "Bash(mkfs:*)",
      "Bash(dd:*)",
      "Bash(rm -rf /*)",
      "Bash(rm -rf ~*)",
      "Bash(git config --global:*)",
      "Bash(git config user.*:*)",
      "Bash(git push --force:*)",
      "Bash(git push -f:*)",
      "Bash(git push --force-with-lease:*)",
      "Bash(git push origin main --force*)",
      "Bash(git push origin master --force*)",
      "Bash(git push origin main -f*)",
      "Bash(git push origin master -f*)",
      {{/* DECIDED: every rule from here down that carries an INTERIOR `*` -- the --no-verify
           pair, the three pipe-to-shell rules, the find/awk execution forms below, and the
           `gh api -X`/`npx -p` families in the ask tier -- is enforced by claude_guard's
           rules.py/judge(), NOT by Claude Code's own matcher. allow-compound-bash.sh did this
           until claude-guard slice 3 cut over and deleted it; rules.matches_glob /
           Rules.whole_glob_defer now route any pattern with an interior wildcard against the
           WHOLE command before it is split into segments, the same thing the hook's
           matches_glob/:78-81,:278 did. Proof both ways in
           home/dot_local/share/claude-guard/tests/test_judge.py:
           test_deny_and_ask_rules_with_an_interior_wildcard_are_globs fires them, and
           test_deny_globs_leave_the_everyday_form_of_each_spawner_allowed proves they do not
           over-match -- both ported case-for-case from the deleted JS file's tests of the
           same two names before this cutover, so this marker is rewritten to name the
           surviving enforcer rather than relocating a proof that already has a home.

           This matters because docs/claude-shell-permissions.md in the server repo measures
           the NATIVE matcher and concludes a flag-level guard "cannot be written at all".
           That is true of the native engine and false of what runs here, and a reviewer has
           already read it as grounds to delete these rules as decorative. They are not.
           Deleting them removes real coverage. */}}
      "Bash(git commit *--no-verify*)",
      "Bash(git commit -n:*)",
      "Bash(git push *--no-verify*)",
      "Bash(* | sh*)",
      "Bash(* | bash*)",
      "Bash(* | zsh*)",
      {{/* claude_guard.rules strips a trailing wildcard, so every allow rule is a bare
           command prefix matched on "prefix + space". find/awk/xargs are everyday tools and
           stay allow-listed, but each takes a command as an argument, so the allow rule alone
           auto-approved `find . -exec id \;`, `awk 'BEGIN{system("id")}'` and `xargs sh -c 'id'`
           with no prompt — the payload is an argument, so no other deny rule ever saw it.
           Deny beats allow in judge_segment, so these cover the execution forms while the
           ordinary search/print/batch forms keep working.

           xargs is no longer covered here: its allow entry is gone (see above), so there is
           no grant left for a deny glob to claw back. find and awk keep theirs because they
           stay allow-listed — their execution forms are a named flag, which a glob CAN pin
           down, where xargs' command word floats after any number of options. */}}
      "Bash(find *-exec*)",
      "Bash(find *-execdir*)",
      "Bash(find *-ok*)",
      "Bash(find *-delete*)",
      "Bash(find *-fprintf*)",
      "Bash(awk *system*)",
      "Edit(.git/**)",
      "Read(~/.config/gcloud/**)",
      "Edit(~/.config/gcloud/**)",
      "Read(~/.config/gh/**)",
      "Edit(~/.config/gh/**)",
      {{/* block-dangerous-bash.sh has denied these to Bash for a long time; the Read/Edit
           side had nothing, so `cat ~/.claude.json` was blocked while Read(~/.claude.json)
           handed over the OAuth token. protect-secrets.sh is the primary gate and gives a
           reason — these are the layer that still stands if the hook is bypassed or its
           jq preflight fails open. */}}
      {{/* The OAuth token store on Linux and WSL — a different file from ~/.claude.json below, and the one the deny list missed. Declared in .chezmoidata/secrets.toml; tests/secret-registry.test.js fails if the dialects drift apart. */}}
      "Read(~/.claude/.credentials.json)",
      "Edit(~/.claude/.credentials.json)",
      "Read(~/.claude.json)",
      "Edit(~/.claude.json)",
      "Read(~/.git-credentials)",
      "Edit(~/.git-credentials)",
      "Read(~/.kube/config)",
      "Edit(~/.kube/config)",
      "Read(~/.docker/config.json)",
      "Edit(~/.docker/config.json)",
      "Read(/etc/shadow)",
      "Read(/etc/gshadow)",
      {{/* Read was denied and Edit was not — surfaced by tests/secret-registry.test.js when the registry made the two dialects comparable. Root-owned, so a write would fail anyway; denied for the same reason the Read is. */}}
      "Edit(/etc/shadow)",
      "Edit(/etc/gshadow)",
      "Read(/proc/*/environ)",
      "Bash(terraform apply:*)",
      "Bash(terraform destroy:*)",
      "Bash(terraform import:*)",
      "Bash(terraform taint:*)",
      "Bash(terraform untaint:*)",
      "Bash(terraform force-unlock:*)",
      "Bash(terraform state rm:*)",
      "Bash(terraform state mv:*)",
      "Bash(terraform state push:*)",
      "Bash(terraform state replace-provider:*)",
      "Bash(terraform workspace delete:*)",
      "Bash(tofu apply:*)",
      "Bash(tofu destroy:*)",
      "Bash(tofu import:*)",
      "Bash(tofu taint:*)",
      "Bash(tofu untaint:*)",
      "Bash(tofu force-unlock:*)",
      "Bash(tofu state rm:*)",
      "Bash(tofu state mv:*)",
      "Bash(tofu state push:*)",
      "Bash(tofu state replace-provider:*)",
      "Bash(tofu workspace delete:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt apply:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt destroy:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt import:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt apply-all:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt destroy-all:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt run-all apply:*)",
      "Bash(terragrunt run-all destroy:*)"
    ],
    "ask": [
      {{/* -t is the short form of --show-token; either one prints the GitHub token into the transcript */}}
      "Bash(gh auth status *--show-token*)",
      "Bash(gh auth status *-t*)",
      "Bash(node -e:*)",
      "Bash(python3 -c:*)",
      "Bash(python -c:*)",
      {{/* A hand-run playbook is a real deploy on the homelab server repo, bypassing that
           repo's scripts/deploy.sh and the lock it takes. These three prefix forms
           (bare, uv run, and uv run --frozen) used to be reached only by accident: the
           uv-python.sh hook prepended an inline `python3 -c '...'` stdio fixup ahead of
           every ansible-playbook invocation, and that prefix matched the
           `Bash(python3 -c:*)` ask rule above, so the whole compound command prompted
           regardless of what followed it. Moving that fixup out to the standalone
           stdio-blocking script above removes the accidental guard, so it is named
           explicitly here instead — the posture on a hand-run playbook must not change
           just because the prefix in front of it did. claude_guard's ansible_readonly_safe()
           narrows this back to unprompted for the genuinely read-only invocation shapes
           (--check, --list-tasks, --list-tags, --list-hosts, --syntax-check with no
           file-valued -e/--extra-vars); everything else still asks. */}}
      "Bash(ansible-playbook:*)",
      "Bash(uv run ansible-playbook:*)",
      "Bash(uv run --frozen ansible-playbook:*)",
      "Bash(npx -y:*)",
      "Bash(npx --yes:*)",
      "Bash(npx -c:*)",
      "Bash(npx --package:*)",
      "Bash(npx --package=*)",
      {{/* -p is the documented short form of --package and fetches an arbitrary package the
           same way; only the long form was listed, while Bash(npx:*) allows the rest. Same
           short-vs-long gap as the `gh auth status -t` / `--show-token` pair above. These
           are prefix rules, so they catch the flag in first position — `npx -y -p pkg` is
           still reached by the -y rule, but a flag order that leads with neither is not
           covered, which is a limit of prefix matching rather than of this entry. */}}
      "Bash(npx -p:*)",
      "Bash(npx -p=*)",
      {{/* The destructive four. These silently destroy work that is NOT in git — uncommitted
           edits, untracked files — which no reflog, no checkpoint and no classifier can bring
           back, so they stay the one Bash tier worth stopping on. Note the cover is PARTIAL:
           a compound like `rm -rf /tmp/x; (...)` was observed running unprompted while a bare
           `rm` prompted, so treat these as a speed bump, not a guarantee. Anything that must
           NEVER run belongs in deny, which is evaluated before both the ask tier and the
           classifier. */}}
      "Bash(rm:*)",
      "Bash(rmdir:*)",
      "Bash(git reset --hard:*)",
      "Bash(git clean:*)",
      "Bash(git restore:*)",
      {{/* Branch-level git is deliberately NOT here any more: checkout/switch/rebase/merge/
           cherry-pick/branch -D/worktree remove are all reflog-recoverable, they were ~27
           prompts a week, and every one of them was approved. Worktree lifecycle in particular
           has to be prompt-free — sessions create, enter and clean up their own worktrees, so a
           prompt there stalls an unattended run for no decision anyone was going to make. */}}
      {{/* stash pop/drop/clear survive the cull for a reason the classifier cannot know: the
           stash stack is SHARED across every worktree and parallel session on this machine, so
           a pop or drop can silently take another session's entry. `push` and `apply` are not
           destructive and are gone. */}}
      "Bash(git stash pop:*)",
      "Bash(git stash drop:*)",
      "Bash(git stash clear)",
      {{/* Merging is what triggers the deploy: the gitops timer pulls origin/master every 30
           min and applies it with Ansible. Kept as the one intentional human checkpoint, now
           that the `master CI gate` ruleset enforces the correctness half. */}}
      "Bash(gh pr merge:*)",
      "Bash(gh api *-X POST*)",
      "Bash(gh api *-X PUT*)",
      "Bash(gh api *-X PATCH*)",
      "Bash(gh api *-X DELETE*)",
      "Bash(gh api *--method POST*)",
      "Bash(gh api *--method PUT*)",
      "Bash(gh api *--method PATCH*)",
      "Bash(gh api *--method DELETE*)",
      "Bash(gh api *-f *)",
      "Bash(gh api *-F *)",
      "Bash(gh api *--input *)",
      "Bash(gh api graphql*)",
      "Bash(gh api */graphql*)",
      {{/* The package-manager and docker install/run rules are gone: they fired ZERO times in a
           measured week. This is an Ansible/kubectl/git homelab, neither cluster node has had
           Docker since 2026-08-14, and dependency installs go through uv/prek. They cost nothing
           to keep, but a rule that never fires still reads as coverage that isn't there. */}}
      {{/* curl/ssh/scp/sftp/rsync/wget were the single biggest source of prompts (~80 a week,
           all approved) and they are gone from this tier on purpose. A prefix rule could never
           narrow them — `Bash(curl http://10.0.0.161/:*)` constrains nothing after the URL, so
           `curl http://10.0.0.161/x -o ~/.ssh/authorized_keys` would have matched it — which is
           exactly the discrimination the auto-mode classifier CAN make, reading the whole line
           against the trusted hosts and domains named in autoMode.environment.

           Consequence worth knowing: an ask rule is what routes a call through the
           PermissionRequest hooks, so claude_guard's curl_safe() / readonly_remote_safe() /
           auto-approve-remote-ssh.sh no longer fast-path these in auto mode — the classifier
           does. claude_guard's checks are retained because they still carry Manual mode,
           where no classifier runs (curl_safe/readonly_remote_safe ran as the standalone
           bash hooks allow-safe-curl.sh/allow-readonly-remote.sh until claude-guard slice 3
           deleted them and moved the same decision into guard-permission-request.sh). */}}
      {{/* the hooks are this config's own enforcement layer — never editable without a prompt (Edit rules also cover Write) */}}
      "Edit(~/.claude/hooks/**)",
      "Edit(~/.claude/settings.json)",
      "Edit(~/.claude/settings.local.json)"
    ],
    "defaultMode": "auto"
  }
